    "id": 7,
    "title": "Modelling stellar light curves: how well can we measure a star's rotation?",
    "description": "The European Space Agency's PLATO mission will measure the rotation of thousands of Sun-like stars from the periodic dimming caused by starspots. But a light curve does not reveal a starspot’s latitude or the star’s inclination, creating an ambiguity in the inferred rotation rate. In this project, you will generate synthetic stellar light curves while varying starspot latitude, size, and lifetime, along with stellar inclination and limb darkening. By recovering the rotation period and comparing it with the known input, you will determine the bias and uncertainty in surface-rotation measurements — and assess how reliably PLATO can constrain the rotation of Sun-like stars. You will also visualise the rotating stellar surface alongside its light curve, making the source of the ambiguity clear. The project provides hands-on experience in Python, forward modelling, parameter recovery, and stellar-rotation physics, with direct relevance to the interpretation of PLATO observations and the internal rotation of Sun-like stars, and would suit a physics, maths or engineering student.",
