    "id": 8,
    "title": "Space weather forecasting: modelling an ensemble of active region evolution",
    "description": "Solar active regions power space weather, but their evolution—and the flares and eruptions they produce—remains difficult to predict. Surface convection continually reshapes themagnetic fields, with different flow patterns producing different outcomes even from the same initial state. In this project, you will writeyour own numerical code to advect magnetic fields through prescribed surface flows. By running ensembles of advection for different flow maps, you will quantify how uncertainty in the flows affects the evolution of polarity separation, tilt angle, and flux cancellation—and identify the fundamental limits of active-region forecasting. You will gain hands-on experience in numerical modelling, scientific programming, ensemble methods, and uncertainty quantification, with the opportunity to compare your results with observations of real solar active regions, and would suit a physics, maths or engineering student.",
