    "title": "Synthesis and characterisation of copper-nickel-manganese alloys",
    "description": "Cu, Ni and Mn can, in theory, be melted and cooled into a solid-solution over a wide range of compositions, a unique property among ternary metal systems. Such an alloy is useful in for structural applications in corrosive environments, situations requiring very high mechanical damping, and in the preparation of nanoporous metals. In practice, improper cooling results in segregation of elements into dendritic microstructures, which affect subsequent processing steps and applications. This project will optimise the induction-furnace-based synthesis of Cu-Ni-Mn alloys, in collaboration with the School of Engineering. The researcher will weigh and mix reagents, explore furnace operating parameters and environments to optimise melting and mixing, and characterise the resultant alloys using X-ray diffraction and scanning electron microscopy. This project is well-suited to a researcher with an interest or background in materials science, engineering, chemistry, or physics.",
