    "title": "Optimised dealloying conditions for robust nanoporous copper",
    "description": "Nanoporous metals consist of a bi-continuous network of nanoscale ligaments and pores. They can have enormous specific surface area, and metallic electrical and thermal conductivity, giving uses in catalysis, as supercapacitors, and in other energy applications. Nanoporous metals are easily prepared by dealloying, where the more reactive metal from a binary alloy is selectively removed. Nanoporous copper can be prepared by dealloying copper-manganese alloys using dilute mineral acids, but there has been little optimisation of the procedure. In this project, the researcher will explore the effect of the dealloying conditions (time, concentration, etc) on the resulting material composition, nanopore geometry, and mechanical robustness. The experiments will be performed in a wet laboratory fume hood, involve handling acids and bulk samples, and will involve characterisation using X-ray diffraction and scanning electron microscopy. This project is well-suited to a researcher with an interest or background in chemistry, physics, or nanomaterials.",
